module lib/hf/sweep
  Run every implementation of a stage on the same input and require them to
  agree. Each member of a registry runs, its result is flattened into one row
  of a table, and the consensus is the elementwise median of the rows. The
  member farthest from the median is blamed, with the flat index of the
  entry where it is farthest, in a report vector. `release_mode()` runs the
  first member alone. One driver exists per member signature, because a Viv
  def is typed by the registry it is called with.

sw_rep_new  @abd99b1d
  why   Every sweep writes its verdict into a report the caller made: the
        blamed member, the flat index, the worst relative deviation and how
        many members ran, at the positions the `SW_REP_*` constants name.

sw_rep_release  @ab8e6817
  why   A caller reads the report the same way after every stage, so a run
        that compared nothing must still leave one that says so: one member,
        no deviation.
  rep  the report vector to overwrite

sw_flat_index  @c4f02769
  why   `take` and `put` read and write an array of any shape through a list of
        flat (row-major) positions; this list lets a driver copy a member's
        result into a row of the table and the consensus back into that shape.
  N     how many entries a member's result has
  ix    the list being filled, `[N]`
  i     the position being written

sw_table_median  @8bc78595
  why   The consensus must not depend on which member is listed first: a
        median of three or more members is right as long as most of them are,
        so a bad member is blamed wherever it sits
        [test "the same bug is blamed wherever it sits in the registry"].
        The deviation is divided by `1 + |median|`, so the tolerance is
        relative for large entries and absolute for small ones.
  T      the table, one row per member, `[nstrat, N]`
  nstrat how many members ran
  N      how many entries each row has
  ncmp   how many leading entries are compared; the rest are copied from row
         `0`
  C      the consensus being written, `[N]`
  rep    the report: blamed member, flat index, worst deviation, member count
  col    one column of the table: every member's value of one entry, sorted
  worst  the largest relative deviation from the median seen so far
  i      the entry being compared
  m      the member being read
  dev    member m's relative deviation from the median at entry i

sw_verdict  @0273bcae
  why   The `sw_check*` drivers return a verdict instead of asserting, so a
        test can require that a planted bug is caught.
  rep   a report a driver filled
  tol   the largest relative deviation that still counts as agreement

sw_run3_arr  @10786f99
  why   The one place the members of a three-argument registry are run and
        compared; `sweep3_arr` and `sw_check3_arr` differ only in what they do
        with the report. Each member gets copies of the inputs (`x1 + 0.0`)
        because a member that writes into an input would otherwise change what
        the next member sees
        [test "a member that writes into its input cannot corrupt the next one"].
  ms       the registry: the members, each `(Arr, Arr, Arr) -> Arr`
  x1       the first input every member gets
  x2       the second input
  x3       the third input
  N        how many entries a member's result has (Viv has no element count)
  ncmp     how many leading entries are compared
  rep      the report to fill
  release  `1` to run member `0` alone and compare nothing
  nstrat   how many members the registry has
  ix       the flat positions `0 .. N-1`
  T        the table of results, one row per member
  out      member `0`'s result; its shape is the shape returned, and its
           entries are overwritten by the consensus
  m        the member being run
  cur      member m's result
  C        the consensus, flat

sweep3_arr  @616c4f0b
  why   The form the library's stages call: in a debug run every member runs
        and a dissent stops the program at the stage that caused it; in a
        release run only the production member runs
        [test "release mode runs member 0 alone and returns it unchanged"].
  ms       the registry of `(Arr, Arr, Arr) -> Arr` members
  x1       the first input
  x2       the second input
  x3       the third input
  N        how many entries a result has
  ncmp     how many leading entries are compared
  tol      the largest relative deviation allowed
  rep      the report, filled for the caller to read
  release  `1` when the program runs in release mode
  R        the consensus, in the members' shape

sw_check3_arr  @27c0ddd5
  why   Tests need to see a dissent without the program stopping, to show a
        planted bug is caught and blamed on the right member and entry
        [test "a bad member listed first is blamed, at flat index 30, and the median is 2"].
  ms    the registry of `(Arr, Arr, Arr) -> Arr` members
  x1    the first input
  x2    the second input
  x3    the third input
  N     how many entries a result has
  ncmp  how many leading entries are compared
  tol   the largest relative deviation that counts as agreement
  rep   the report to fill
  out   where the consensus is copied, any array with `N` entries
  R     the consensus
  ix    the flat positions `0 .. N-1`

sw_run5_arr  @4eab1a07
  what  Run every member of a five-argument registry (the J/K builders'
        signature) on copies of the same inputs, or member `0` alone when
        `release` is `1`, and return the elementwise median in the members'
        shape, with the dissent recorded in `rep`.
  why   The same work as `sw_run3_arr` for members that take five arrays. A Viv
        def is typed by the registry signature it is called with, so each
        signature needs its own driver
        [test "the J/K driver compares [2, n, n] results and blames the bad K"].
  ms       the registry: members `(Arr, Arr, Arr, Arr, Arr) -> Arr`
  x1       the first input
  x2       the second input
  x3       the third input
  x4       the fourth input (for J/K, the densities)
  x5       the fifth input (for J/K, the options)
  N        how many entries a result has
  ncmp     how many leading entries are compared
  rep      the report to fill
  release  `1` to run member `0` alone
  nstrat   how many members the registry has
  ix       the flat positions `0 .. N-1`
  T        the table of results, one row per member
  out      member `0`'s result, overwritten by the consensus
  m        the member being run
  cur      member m's result
  C        the consensus, flat

